  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIn the north/northwest flow aloft on the back-side of the mid and \nupper level trough that has moved downstream over the Canadian \nmaritimes cool and breezy conditions persist with mostly cloudy \nconditions, except south and east of the Greater Capital Region. A \nsurface trough and a mid-level disturbance will move across the \nregion this afternoon into early tonight. A few flurries or \nsprinkles may impact the western Mohawk Valley, western Dacks and \neastern Catskills this afternoon, but no accumulation is expected. \nWest/northwest winds will continue with some gusts 20-30 mph due\nto some better mixing in the Hudson River Valley and the eastern\nMohawk Valley before the clouds filled back in. The \nmoisture/clouds look to get trapped under an inversion tonight, \nas the boundary layer flow shifts from northwest/north to \nnortherly with mostly cloudy conditions continuing from the \nCapital Region/Berkshires north and west. Lows will range from \nthe upper 20s to mid 30s. \n\nA 1035 hPa or so surface anticylone begins to ridge in from the\nMidwest and Ohio Valley to open the week. Mid and upper level \nheights begin to rise above normal from west to east on Monday, \ndue to a strong ridge over the central CONUS. Partly to mostly \ncloudy conditions will continue to persist with the clouds more \nprevalent over the higher terrain by the late morning into the \nearly afternoon. High temps will still run slightly below normal\nwith mid and upper 40s mainly in the valleys and 30s to around \n40F over the mountains with breezy conditions. The strong \nsurface anticyclone continues to be in control from the Piedmont\nRegion with near ideal radiational cooling conditions expected \nwith lows in the 20s to around 30F. Some cirrus may be moving \ninto locations west of the Hudson River Valley late. Tuesday, \ntemps rise near to slightly above normal with more sunshine with\nthe subsidence from the mid and upper-level ridge and the broad\nsurface anticylone building in from the south/southwest. Expect\nhighs 50 to 55F in the valleys and 40s over the higher terrain.\nTemps look to run about 5-10 degrees above normal for the mid \nto latter portion of the week with a fair amount of sunshine due\nto strong subsidence with the ridge. A moisture starved cold \nfront may increase clouds late Friday into early Saturday,\nespecially north of I-90, but overall dry weather is expected \ninto next weekend with temps still running above normal.",
